				<!-- BOTH 401 AND 307 OPEN THE PICKER, AND BOTH ARE OFFERED ON EVERY
				     ITEM. Do not re-add a hasSubmenu visibility gate here. That gate
				     hid the friendly way to repoint a menu item on 8 of the 12 Home
				     rows.

				     307 USED to be a raw keyboard prefilled with an ActivateWindow
				     string, which nobody edits by hand. It is not any more, and the
				     change is in the ADD-ON, not here: the menu system is now
				     script.estuary8.shortcuts, our own fork, whose _choose_action
				     (pickers.py) sends 307 to the same picker 401 uses whenever the
				     skin declares groupings, and to the old keyboard only when it
				     declares none. This skin declares them, in shortcuts/menus.xml.

				     So the two labels stay exactly where they are. "Change action" is
				     still on 307 and "Choose item for menu" is still on 401, matching
				     Estuary 7 one for one, which is the standard for both skins. The
				     button that reads "Change action" now BEHAVES the way Estuary 7's
				     does, which is what was actually wrong. Do not swap these labels
				     to work around behaviour; that was tried and it broke the match.

				     Control 401 is CONTROL_CHOOSE_SHORTCUT (base.py:42) and it runs
				     _choose_shortcut (pickers.py:153). The handler does more than set
				     an action: pickers.py:184-208 calls set_label UNGUARDED, then
				     set_icon, set_visible, and then, whenever the chosen shortcut's
				     submenu differs from the item's, calls set_submenu FOLLOWED BY
				     drop_per_item_submenu, and manager.py:143-151 shows that second
				     call does `del self.working[submenu_key]`.

				     That branch is now controlled from the SKIN, in menus.xml.
				     pickers.py:200-208 resolves the picked shortcut's `name` through
				     config.get_default_menu, which searches every <menu> and
				     <submenu> in menus.xml. The <group name="sections"> block there
				     declares one action-mode shortcut per Home row whose name is
				     EXACTLY the submenu template name, so picking Movies on the
				     Movies row computes new_submenu == "movies" == previous_submenu
				     and the destructive branch is never entered. Picking a different
				     section carries that section's submenu across instead of deleting
				     one. Measured against the real 3.0.1 MenuManager: 0 of 144
				     item/leaf combinations preserved a submenu before that block
				     existed, 64 do now, including all 8 same-section picks.

				     Picks that still clear the submenu do so correctly: a browse
				     path, an add-on or a typed action is not a library section and
				     has no submenu to inherit. pickers.py:707-719 renames any
				     browse-mode pick to browse-{hash}, so a browse leaf can never
				     carry a template name; that is upstream's design, not a gap here.

				     Do NOT name the legacy line's version token when re-editing this
				     comment. estuary8/tools/check_skinshortcuts_floor.py rejects any
				     such token anywhere in the shipped tree, INCLUDING prose, and
				     build.py runs it before writing a zip. The wording this file
				     carried at 0.1.16 failed that guard on this very line, which is
				     why the sentences above are phrased around the token rather than
				     restored verbatim. Rewording it back is a build failure, not a
				     tidy-up. -->
				<control type="button" id="401">
					<description>Reads "Choose item for menu" (32048). Opens the shortcut picker, whose first entry is the "Main menu" section list. Offered on every item; see the comment above.</description>
					<include>DefaultSettingButton</include>
					<label>$ADDON[script.estuary8.shortcuts 32048]</label>
					<label2>$INFO[Container(211).ListItem.Label]</label2>
					<visible>!String.IsEqual(Container(211).ListItem.Property(defaultID),libreelec)</visible>
				</control>
				<control type="button" id="307">
					<description>Reads "Change action" (32027). Opens the shortcut picker, via _choose_action in script.estuary8.shortcuts, because this skin declares groupings. Raw keyboard entry is still reachable from the item context menu as "Edit Action" (32172). These two labels match Estuary 7 one for one and are not to be swapped back.</description>
					<include>DefaultSettingButton</include>
					<label>$ADDON[script.estuary8.shortcuts 32027]</label>
					<!-- pathLabel, not path. `path` is the machine string, and it put
					     "videodb://movies/titles/" on screen where Estuary 7 shows a
					     readable name. Estuary 7 binds Property(displaypath), which
					     does NOT exist in this add-on line and would render blank, so
					     the fork provides the readable value under the name its own
					     convention dictates: {base}Label, as in widgetLabel and
					     backgroundLabel. describe_path() in constants.py builds it and
					     falls back to the raw path, so this can never be emptier than
					     what it replaced. -->
					<label2>$INFO[Container(211).ListItem.Property(pathLabel)]</label2>
					<visible>!String.IsEqual(Container(211).ListItem.Property(defaultID),libreelec)</visible>
