				<!-- 312 and 311 ARE THE TWO RECOVERY BUTTONS. Both were declared or
				     implied by the dialog code and neither could be pressed.

				     312 is CONTROL_RESET_ITEM (base.py:40) and it is the ONLY binding
				     for _reset_current_item (base.py:599-600). Until 2026-07-31 it was
				     declared here with an empty <label> and an unconditional
				     <visible>false</visible>, so it rendered as nothing: measured on a
				     running bench, Control.IsVisible(312) was False and
				     Control.GetLabel(312) was empty. The item context menu
				     (items.py:448-454) offers Change label, Edit Action, Change
				     thumbnail, Edit submenu and Delete, and no reset, so there was no
				     second route to it either. It is exposed because the 0.1.26 note
				     tells anyone hit by the 0.1.25 "Change action" defect to press it.
				     A remediation the user cannot press is not a remediation.

				     311 is CONTROL_RESTORE_DELETED (base.py:39), bound to
				     _restore_deleted_item (base.py:597-598), and was never declared in
				     this file at all. It is the only route back for a row that was
				     DELETED rather than damaged, and 312 cannot do that job:
				     manager.reset_item returns False when the item is absent from the
				     working copy (manager.py:345-346), and _reset_current_item reaches
				     it only via _get_selected_item, so a row that is gone cannot even
				     be selected to reset.

				     311 recovers across restarts, which is why it is worth exposing
				     rather than treating deletion as permanent. get_removed_items
				     (manager.py:491-518) does not read a session cache; it diffs the
				     skin defaults in shortcuts/menus.xml against the working copy
				     loaded from userdata.json, so any default row missing from the
				     saved menu is offered back however long ago it was removed.

				     NEITHER BUTTON IS MOVED AND NEITHER IS RENAMED. 312 stays in the
				     slot it already occupied, below the widget and submenu rows and
				     above 10005, and takes the label the add-on already carries for
				     it, 32138 "Reset Item". 311 takes 32137 "Restore Deleted Item"
				     and is placed next to it so the two recovery actions sit together
				     at the end of the pane, after the everyday edit buttons. This is
				     a grouplist (90001) with a pagecontrol, so it scrolls and there is
				     no per-control focus wiring to disturb.

				     311's gate is the add-on's own property. _update_deleted_property
				     (base.py:543-546) sets skinshortcuts-hasdeleted to "true" exactly
				     when has_removed_items is true, so the button appears only when
				     there is something to restore and never as a dead row. That
				     property existed and this skin was the only thing not reading it.

				     KNOWN AND DELIBERATE, do not report as a regression: pressing 312
				     on a menu that has no skin default, i.e. a user-created menu,
				     confirms and then silently does nothing, because reset_item
				     returns False and _reset_current_item (items.py:346-365) does not
				     notify on that path. That is the add-on's existing behaviour, it
				     is not new here, and it was left alone rather than widened into a
				     Python change. -->
